According to Judge Thomas Cooley, it is easier to tell what a constitution is not than what it is. It is not the beginning of a community, nor the origin of private rights; it is not the fountain of law, not the recipient state of government, it is not the cause but the consequence of personal and political freedom;
It grants no rights to the people, but is the creature of their power, the instrument of their protection in the employment of the rights and powers which they possessed before the constitution was made;
It is the framework of the political government and is necessarily based upon the pre-existing condition of laws, rights, habits, and codes of thought. There is nothing primitive in it; it is all derived from a known source. It presupposes an organized society, law, order, property, personal freedom, a love of political liberty, and enough cultivated intelligence to know how to guard it against the encroachments of tyranny.
